      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">Palantir's interview process — stage by stage</h2>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Application and resume screen.</strong> Palantir hires from a wide range of backgrounds — elite universities, military veterans, and strong self-taught engineers. The common signal is intellectual horsepower and a track record of consequential work. Generic resumes get filtered out; resumes that show ownership, impact, and complexity survive.</li>
        <li><strong>Recruiter screen.</strong> Background, role interest, and basic technical fit. Also an initial probe for mission alignment — Palantir's government and defense work is controversial, and they want candidates who have engaged with it seriously rather than being surprised by it.</li>
        <li><strong>Hacker round (coding).</strong> 60–90 minutes of live coding that is more conversational than standard interviews. The interviewer will interrupt with follow-up questions, ask you to extend the problem, and discuss alternative approaches. Strong hacker round performance combines clean code, clear reasoning, and confident communication under ambiguity.</li>
        <li><strong>Decomp round (system/product design).</strong> A product or systems design problem where you are asked to break down a complex scenario. For FDE candidates, this may include a client scenario. Palantir expects structured thinking, explicit tradeoff reasoning, and clear communication of what you would build first and why.</li>
        <li><strong>Final loop.</strong> Behavioral and culture rounds with team members and often a senior leader. Expect direct questions about your views on Palantir's mission and work in government, defense, and law enforcement — vague or evasive answers signal poor fit.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="content-panel">
      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">Preparation strategy by track</h2>
      <h3>Software Engineering / Forward Deployed Engineer</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Hacker round prep: practice coding problems at medium difficulty with a focus on explaining your reasoning out loud — the communication matters as much as the solution</li>
        <li>Decomp prep: practice designing systems with explicit tradeoffs — "I would do X first because of Y, and defer Z because of W" is the format Palantir rewards</li>
        <li>FDE-specific: prepare client communication stories — examples where you translated technical complexity into business clarity for non-technical stakeholders</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 style="margin-top:20px;">All tracks: mission engagement</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Research Palantir's actual work: Gotham (government/defense), Foundry (commercial), Apollo (deployment infrastructure), AIP (AI platform)</li>
        <li>Form a genuine view on the ethical questions around government data and defense work — interviewers will probe for authentic engagement, not rehearsed neutrality</li>
        <li>Prepare to articulate why you specifically want to work on consequential, high-stakes problems rather than consumer products</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 style="margin-top:20px;">Behavioral preparation</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Prepare 5–7 STAR stories emphasizing ownership, speed of delivery, and working in ambiguous or high-pressure environments</li>
        <li>Stories about working with difficult stakeholders, navigating constraints, and making high-stakes decisions resonate strongly at Palantir</li>
